<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiglath-Pileser I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1114–1076 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiglath-Pileser I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from about 1114 to 1076 BCE. He campaigned widely, fighting the Mushki and the Nairi lands in the north, reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, where Phoenician cities such as Arwad paid tribute, and raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. His annals, written on clay prisms, are among the fullest royal inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian</a> period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i.jpg" alt="Tiglath-Pileser I" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lehmann-Haupt, Carl Friedrich, 1861-1938 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_of_Tiglath-Pileser_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
